PUBLIC RESPONSE: C

The circumflex accent (ˆ) has a well-documented range of uses across the history of French orthography, both in its standardization and in earlier/variable scribal practice. These include:

- marking loss of a following sibilant (H), e.g. *forest* → *forêt*, *paste* → *pâte*;
- signaling reduction of a former diphthong or hiatus sequence (G, I), e.g. *aoust* → *août*, *traïtre* → *traître*;
- disambiguating homophones (B), e.g. *du* vs. *dû*, *sur* vs. *sûr*;
- indicating a difference in vowel timbre/quality that can be described in terms of open vs. closed (A), e.g. *pote* [pɔt] vs. *pôte* [pot];
- historically marking vowel length (D), e.g. the long vowel in *mûr* or *île*.

Option F is also attested in a handful of 16th–17th-century learned spellings where the accent was added for visual or etymological prestige (e.g., certain humanist editions of *âcre*, *poëte*).  

By contrast, the circumflex has never been systematically or even sporadically employed simply to signal that a vowel continues a Classical Latin /o/ (option C). When ô appears, its motivation is one of the other functions listed above; the mere etymological provenance of the vowel is not a documented trigger for the accent. Therefore C is the function that has never been attested.

